1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,576
Here is a scene that asks
Python a simple question:

2
00:00:03,576 --> 00:00:05,132
where am I running?

3
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:07,802
Run it on a laptop, and it
says something like darwin,

4
00:00:07,802 --> 00:00:11,691
or linux, or windows, and
names the computer's processor.

5
00:00:11,691 --> 00:00:16,440
Run it in ManimOnline, and it
gives a much stranger answer.

6
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:18,866
Platform: emscripten.

7
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:21,688
Machine: wasm thirty two.

8
00:00:21,688 --> 00:00:25,774
Those words mean this Python isn't
running on your operating system at all.

9
00:00:25,774 --> 00:00:27,945
It's running inside your web browser,

10
00:00:27,945 --> 00:00:33,391
inside a single tab, on an imaginary
processor that the browser provides.

11
00:00:33,391 --> 00:00:35,585
This episode is about how that works,

12
00:00:35,585 --> 00:00:39,617
and about the surprisingly
delicate problem of stopping it.

13
00:00:39,617 --> 00:00:44,307
The obvious way to build a website that
runs Manim is to put Manim on a server.

14
00:00:44,307 --> 00:00:47,756
Your browser sends code;
the server renders a video;

15
00:00:47,756 --> 00:00:49,102
the video comes back.

16
00:00:49,102 --> 00:00:52,040
But think about what that
server is agreeing to do.

17
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,511
Run arbitrary code, written
by anyone on the internet.

18
00:00:55,511 --> 00:00:58,123
That code could loop
forever, fill the disk,

19
00:00:58,123 --> 00:01:00,643
or try to attack the
machine it's running on.

20
00:01:00,643 --> 00:01:03,615
So every job would need
a locked-down sandbox,

21
00:01:03,615 --> 00:01:07,423
limits on time and memory,
and storage for every result.

22
00:01:07,423 --> 00:01:09,420
And every render costs real money,

23
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,115
for every visitor, forever.

24
00:01:11,115 --> 00:01:13,321
ManimOnline is a static site:

25
00:01:13,321 --> 00:01:15,921
a folder of files hosted on GitHub Pages.

26
00:01:15,921 --> 00:01:18,185
There is no server-side program at all.

27
00:01:18,185 --> 00:01:20,298
So the Python has to run somewhere else.

28
00:01:20,298 --> 00:01:24,850
And the only computer available is
the one the visitor already has.

29
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:26,348
The trick is WebAssembly.

30
00:01:26,348 --> 00:01:30,144
Browsers can run a compact, low-level
kind of program called WebAssembly,

31
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:32,989
safely sandboxed, and nearly
as fast as native code.

32
00:01:32,989 --> 00:01:35,937
And the standard Python
interpreter is itself a program,

33
00:01:35,937 --> 00:01:39,954
written in C. Compile that C to
WebAssembly instead of to your laptop's

34
00:01:39,954 --> 00:01:43,705
processor, and you get a Python
interpreter that runs inside a web page.

35
00:01:43,705 --> 00:01:45,829
That project is called Pyodide.

36
00:01:45,829 --> 00:01:47,675
Those strange words from the opening,

37
00:01:47,675 --> 00:01:50,067
emscripten and wasm thirty two,

38
00:01:50,067 --> 00:01:54,188
are the names of the compiler toolchain
and of the imaginary processor.

39
00:01:54,188 --> 00:01:55,605
The cost is a download.

40
00:01:55,605 --> 00:01:57,358
The interpreter is several megabytes,

41
00:01:57,358 --> 00:02:00,538
which is why the first render on
your first visit takes a while.

42
00:02:00,538 --> 00:02:03,011
After that, the browser keeps a copy.

43
00:02:03,011 --> 00:02:05,560
Libraries are downloaded
only when they're needed.

44
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:08,387
ManimOnline looks at your
code before running it,

45
00:02:08,387 --> 00:02:11,916
and if it mentions NumPy,
it fetches NumPy first.

46
00:02:11,916 --> 00:02:15,399
This scene says n p, so NumPy is loaded.

47
00:02:15,399 --> 00:02:17,988
A scene that never mentions
it doesn't pay for it.

48
00:02:17,988 --> 00:02:20,113
The same goes for a few other packages,

49
00:02:20,113 --> 00:02:21,808
like the one that lays out graphs.

50
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:23,212
And what about Manim itself?

51
00:02:23,212 --> 00:02:25,035
That's a story for next episode.

52
00:02:25,035 --> 00:02:25,988
The short version:

53
00:02:25,988 --> 00:02:29,215
the real Manim depends on libraries
that aren't available here,

54
00:02:29,215 --> 00:02:31,200
so ManimOnline brings its own engine,

55
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:35,067
written in plain Python,
that runs inside Pyodide.

56
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:36,147
Now the delicate part.

57
00:02:36,147 --> 00:02:39,003
A web page's main thread is
like a kitchen with one cook.

58
00:02:39,003 --> 00:02:41,104
Clicks, key presses, animations,

59
00:02:41,104 --> 00:02:43,264
drawing: everything is an order ticket,

60
00:02:43,264 --> 00:02:45,377
and the cook handles them one at a time.

61
00:02:45,377 --> 00:02:47,525
That's fine as long as
each order is quick.

62
00:02:47,525 --> 00:02:49,556
But Python code can take a long time.

63
00:02:49,556 --> 00:02:50,834
And it can take forever:

64
00:02:50,834 --> 00:02:52,471
a loop that never ends.

65
00:02:52,471 --> 00:02:54,003
If that ran on the main thread,

66
00:02:54,003 --> 00:02:56,012
the cook would be stuck stirring one pot,

67
00:02:56,012 --> 00:02:57,556
and the whole page would freeze.

68
00:02:57,556 --> 00:02:59,321
You couldn't even click
a button to stop it.

69
00:02:59,321 --> 00:03:02,177
So ManimOnline gives Python its own cook,

70
00:03:02,177 --> 00:03:03,349
in its own kitchen:

71
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:05,555
a Web Worker, a separate thread.

72
00:03:05,555 --> 00:03:08,608
The page stays responsive while
the worker runs your scene.

73
00:03:08,608 --> 00:03:09,769
Here's the subtle part.

74
00:03:09,769 --> 00:03:12,707
You might think: just set
a timer inside the worker,

75
00:03:12,707 --> 00:03:14,564
and stop after ninety seconds.

76
00:03:14,564 --> 00:03:17,281
But that timer is also
just an order ticket,

77
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:20,578
and it joins the queue behind
the Python code that's running.

78
00:03:20,578 --> 00:03:24,653
If that code never finishes, the worker
never gets round to reading the timer.

79
00:03:24,653 --> 00:03:27,498
A timeout inside a busy
worker can't interrupt it.

80
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:31,201
The only one who can stop the
worker is someone outside it.

81
00:03:31,201 --> 00:03:33,233
So the main page keeps the stopwatch.

82
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:36,275
When ninety seconds pass,
or you press Cancel render,

83
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:39,259
or you edit the code so the
old result no longer matters,

84
00:03:39,259 --> 00:03:41,557
the page simply terminates the worker,

85
00:03:41,557 --> 00:03:43,949
and starts a new one for the next render.

86
00:03:43,949 --> 00:03:45,923
That's why Cancel render is instant,

87
00:03:45,923 --> 00:03:47,641
even for code that's stuck.

88
00:03:47,641 --> 00:03:51,356
The trade-off is that a terminated
worker loses its loaded Python,

89
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:55,183
so the next render takes a
few seconds to start it again.

90
00:03:55,183 --> 00:03:58,515
Two more pieces of care
make the editor trustworthy.

91
00:03:58,515 --> 00:04:00,733
First, every render starts fresh.

92
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:02,555
Your code runs in a brand new,

93
00:04:02,555 --> 00:04:07,234
empty namespace, with ManimOnline's
library definitions rebuilt.

94
00:04:07,234 --> 00:04:11,286
So nothing left over from an earlier
version of your code can quietly affect

95
00:04:11,286 --> 00:04:14,363
this one. If it works now, it's
because of what's on the screen.

96
00:04:14,363 --> 00:04:16,627
Second, answers can arrive out of order.

97
00:04:16,627 --> 00:04:18,519
Suppose you edit, a render starts,

98
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:19,599
and then you edit again.

99
00:04:19,599 --> 00:04:22,177
The second render might
finish before the first.

100
00:04:22,177 --> 00:04:26,136
Without care, the late, outdated
result could overwrite the newer one.

101
00:04:26,136 --> 00:04:29,050
So every render is stamped
with a revision number,

102
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:32,626
and the page only accepts results
that match the current revision.

103
00:04:32,626 --> 00:04:35,006
Anything older is quietly thrown away.

104
00:04:35,006 --> 00:04:38,535
The same rule protects loads from
the gallery and from HashBin:

105
00:04:38,535 --> 00:04:42,600
if you've typed since
asking, your newer edits win.

106
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:44,992
So what does the worker actually produce?

107
00:04:44,992 --> 00:04:46,884
Not a video. Frames.

108
00:04:46,884 --> 00:04:50,924
As your scene runs, every play
and every wait advances a clock,

109
00:04:50,924 --> 00:04:53,235
fifteen times per second of animation.

110
00:04:53,235 --> 00:04:56,520
At each tick, the engine takes a
snapshot of everything on screen:

111
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,203
every shape's points, colours,

112
00:04:58,203 --> 00:04:59,573
opacity and position.

113
00:04:59,573 --> 00:05:00,990
This scene makes that visible:

114
00:05:00,990 --> 00:05:04,635
an updater drops a little dot at the
moving dot's position on every frame,

115
00:05:04,635 --> 00:05:05,994
so you can literally count them.

116
00:05:05,994 --> 00:05:08,003
Four seconds, about sixty dots.

117
00:05:08,003 --> 00:05:10,510
Each snapshot is written
as structured data:

118
00:05:10,510 --> 00:05:14,005
a tree of objects, which is sent
from the worker to the page.

119
00:05:14,005 --> 00:05:15,329
That could be a lot of data.

120
00:05:15,329 --> 00:05:17,732
A two-minute scene is
eighteen hundred frames,

121
00:05:17,732 --> 00:05:19,984
and many of them contain
hundreds of shapes.

122
00:05:19,984 --> 00:05:23,456
But most of a scene doesn't
change from one frame to the next.

123
00:05:23,456 --> 00:05:26,440
So before sending,
identical pieces are pooled:

124
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:28,692
an unchanged shape is sent once,

125
00:05:28,692 --> 00:05:31,943
and every frame that contains
it simply refers back to it.

126
00:05:31,943 --> 00:05:37,097
In 3D, a shape that only turns can
even be sent as "the same as before,

127
00:05:37,097 --> 00:05:39,884
rotated". Even so, there's a ceiling:

128
00:05:39,884 --> 00:05:42,554
results over thirty two
megabytes are refused,

129
00:05:42,554 --> 00:05:46,967
and that's one reason for the
preview-length limits you met last episode.

130
00:05:46,967 --> 00:05:51,390
On the page, a renderer
turns each frame into S V G,

131
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:54,455
the vector picture format
browsers draw natively.

132
00:05:54,455 --> 00:05:57,195
Every outline becomes an S V G path,

133
00:05:57,195 --> 00:05:59,239
and its commands are, once again,

134
00:05:59,239 --> 00:06:01,352
cubic Bézier curves.

135
00:06:01,352 --> 00:06:03,349
Episode four all the way down.

136
00:06:03,349 --> 00:06:06,472
Because every frame is computed
before playback starts,

137
00:06:06,472 --> 00:06:08,202
the player is just a flipbook.

138
00:06:08,202 --> 00:06:09,479
Seeking is instant:

139
00:06:09,479 --> 00:06:11,105
jump to any frame and draw it.

140
00:06:11,105 --> 00:06:13,635
Formulas need one extra round trip.

141
00:06:13,635 --> 00:06:15,818
Python can't measure MathJax glyphs,

142
00:06:15,818 --> 00:06:18,070
because MathJax lives in the page.

143
00:06:18,070 --> 00:06:22,668
So on the first pass, the engine
estimates the size of each new formula,

144
00:06:22,668 --> 00:06:24,375
and lists the ones it guessed.

145
00:06:24,375 --> 00:06:26,581
The page typesets and measures them,

146
00:06:26,581 --> 00:06:29,425
then asks for a second
render with the real sizes.

147
00:06:29,425 --> 00:06:32,839
That's why the boxes around
these formulas fit so snugly.

148
00:06:32,839 --> 00:06:35,033
A guess wouldn't.

149
00:06:35,033 --> 00:06:37,227
Finally, exporting a video.

150
00:06:37,227 --> 00:06:39,700
Modern browsers include a video encoder,

151
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:43,857
available to web pages through
an interface called WebCodecs.

152
00:06:43,857 --> 00:06:47,212
The page draws each frame,
hands it to the encoder,

153
00:06:47,212 --> 00:06:49,255
and receives compressed video.

154
00:06:49,255 --> 00:06:51,066
One detail matters a lot.

155
00:06:51,066 --> 00:06:52,611
Each frame needs a timestamp:

156
00:06:52,611 --> 00:06:54,236
when it should appear in the video.

157
00:06:54,236 --> 00:06:56,651
You might be tempted to
use the clock on the wall,

158
00:06:56,651 --> 00:06:58,752
the time each frame was encoded.

159
00:06:58,752 --> 00:07:00,692
But encoding speed varies:

160
00:07:00,692 --> 00:07:02,689
some frames are harder than others,

161
00:07:02,689 --> 00:07:04,825
and your computer is doing other things.

162
00:07:04,825 --> 00:07:07,727
Wall-clock timestamps would
make the video stutter.

163
00:07:07,727 --> 00:07:12,162
So ManimOnline computes every
timestamp from the frame number alone:

164
00:07:12,162 --> 00:07:15,575
frame forty five appears
at exactly three seconds.

165
00:07:15,575 --> 00:07:17,456
This scene's clock is a good test.

166
00:07:17,456 --> 00:07:19,755
Export it, scrub through the video,

167
00:07:19,755 --> 00:07:23,006
and the readout always
agrees with the video player.

168
00:07:23,006 --> 00:07:27,928
The encoded frames are then
packaged into an MP4 or WebM file,

169
00:07:27,928 --> 00:07:29,844
entirely in memory.

170
00:07:29,844 --> 00:07:31,202
In the MP4,

171
00:07:31,202 --> 00:07:35,208
the index that describes where every
frame lives is placed before the frame

172
00:07:35,208 --> 00:07:39,364
data, so a player can start showing the
video without reading the whole file

173
00:07:39,364 --> 00:07:41,033
first.

174
00:07:41,033 --> 00:07:43,877
Let's follow one render
all the way through.

175
00:07:43,877 --> 00:07:48,881
You stop typing. The page sends your code
to a worker running Python compiled to

176
00:07:48,881 --> 00:07:52,028
WebAssembly, with a
stopwatch held outside.

177
00:07:52,028 --> 00:07:56,486
The code runs in a fresh namespace and
produces a snapshot fifteen times a

178
00:07:56,486 --> 00:07:59,958
second. Identical pieces are
pooled and sent to the page,

179
00:07:59,958 --> 00:08:03,673
which ignores anything stale, draws S V G,

180
00:08:03,673 --> 00:08:06,181
measures formulas, and plays the flipbook.

181
00:08:06,181 --> 00:08:07,783
And if you ask for a video,

182
00:08:07,783 --> 00:08:11,893
frames are encoded with timestamps
computed from frame numbers alone.

183
00:08:11,893 --> 00:08:13,809
But we skipped the biggest question.

184
00:08:13,809 --> 00:08:16,676
If the real Manim can't
run in the browser,

185
00:08:16,676 --> 00:08:18,662
what exactly is drawing these frames?

186
00:08:18,662 --> 00:08:21,262
And how could anyone know
that it behaves the same?

187
00:08:21,262 --> 00:08:22,272
That's next time.
